            <div xml:id="d428">

                <pb facs="0410" n="345" sameAs="frus:frus1936v03/pg345"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.9111/467: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Iran</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hornibrook</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Teheran</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-01-02T18:00:00+03:25">January 2, 1936—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1. Editorial appearing in today’s <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Setareh-Ye-Djehane</hi>, which is owned by a deputy in the Majlis, takes strong
                    exception to alleged undignified and frivolous manner in which Djalal incident
                    has been treated by the American press. Article alludes to recall of Djalal and
                    opines that this is insufficient and there is nothing in principle to prevent
                    dissolving Imperial Legation in Washington, recall of the entire <pb facs="0411"
                        n="346" xml:id="pg346"/> staff and the rupture of diplomatic relations. I
                    view this article with concern since, in view of close relations between the
                    Iranian press and the Government, the article may possibly presage action of
                    kind described. Article undoubtedly inspired in the main by colored Djalal mail
                    report.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hornibrook</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
